 ],
 "literal": "With tops where rocks and hillocks meet, / like the eight-day moon, the curved bank / of the clear-water small pond is breaking, alas! / He of the sharp spear and accumulated strength, / chariot-generous Pari's cool Parampu country!",
 "translation": "With peaks where rocks and hillocks meet, like the eight-day crescent moon, the curved bank of the clear-water small pond is breached, alas! Such is the fate of the cool Parampu country of Pari, who had sharp spears, mighty shoulders, and gave away chariots.",
 "modern": "பாறைகளும் குன்றுகளும் ஒன்றோடொன்று பொருந்திய உச்சிகளை உடையது; எட்டாம் நாள் பிறை நிலவைப் போன்ற வளைந்த கரையை உடைய தெளிந்த நீரைக் கொண்ட சிறு குளத்தின் கரை உடைந்து நீர் வீணாவது போல, கூர்மையான வேலையும் திரண்ட தோள் வலிமையையும் தேர்களைக் கொடையாக வழங்கும் வள்ளல் தன்மையையும் உடைய பாரியினது குளிர்ந்த பறம்பு நாடு இன்று சிதைந்து அழிகின்றதே, அந்தோ!",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the poet Kapilar after the death of his close friend and patron, King Pari. It belongs to the Potuviyal tiṇai and Kaiyaṟunilai tuṟai, which expresses grief over the death of a great person. Kapilar laments that the beautiful Parampu country, once prosperous and secure under Pari's rule, is now being ruined and divided by enemies, just like a small pond of clear water whose curved bank, shaped like an eight-day crescent moon, is breached and its water lost. The comparison of the curved hill country to the crescent moon and the breaching of the pond to the destruction of the land vividly conveys the deep sorrow of loss. Key words include 'பாரி' (Pari), 'பறம்பு நாடு' (Parampu country), and 'கீள்வது' (being breached/broken).",
